#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358199 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc1fbb99-83e1-52ba-83f1-367de07351a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Improper Integral Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a68e9057-3a03-5622-bab7-800446dddbfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358201} {:answer/id #uuid "cd1b2a10-72b7-51fd-b954-032a18933ab6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358202} {:answer/id #uuid "d43b0b2a-9cc2-5192-b605-d760a603eb0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358203} {:answer/id #uuid "4f90053a-0bbe-5121-87fc-21e92a1725a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358204} {:answer/id #uuid "85e6f8b2-f816-52fb-9e8f-ee3cd0a535c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f90053a-0bbe-5121-87fc-21e92a1725a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358204} :answer-field/id #uuid "094f57d4-2649-5750-89e1-6712b8da9f29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358200}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9aeeaa0f-307c-589b-84c4-fc6bc50fe634" :question/math-academy-id "q-152255" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{-\\sqrt{x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=\\sqrt{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,⟹\\,2\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\sqrt{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{-\\sqrt{x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{3}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{-\\sqrt{x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{3}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{3}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{3}^{-u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{3}^{-u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{3}^{a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{3}^{x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{3}^{x}}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{3}^{x}}=0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067475/assets/ff41c6fa5858f45e76a4d4fec0f0a8080300b69fcb632df60141d93c66f3cdd9.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{-\\sqrt{x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{3}^{a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358206 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e84d5ab0-cec4-5988-9db3-8e296590d279" :knowledge-point/title "Improper Integral Involving Other Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4064223c-ad38-598c-abdb-84c9c7518a0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358208} {:answer/id #uuid "36a1677c-6a52-5112-ac7a-da0f9975fff0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358209} {:answer/id #uuid "1d6f2683-aad9-5c97-8ccc-613e305b9d74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358210} {:answer/id #uuid "1cf49e73-6903-5817-8ca0-2ca2215bbe52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358211} {:answer/id #uuid "aa56137b-0d0d-5428-85a1-5d6a3bc39b54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358212}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4064223c-ad38-598c-abdb-84c9c7518a0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358208} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c568c03-b02e-5eda-9299-623bd307d4bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358207}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "765ece30-2a64-5577-838d-0462df1cc263" :question/math-academy-id "q-152874" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{2}{3}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{2}{3}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{2}{3}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{3}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}]}_{a}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{3}^{x}]}_{a}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({3}^{2}-{3}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\cdot (9-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{3}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={3}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{3}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{3}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067475/assets/abae6131aceb7348a82ea01a544b28a62d8835b7857da14a6339b4ae3b88a1fd.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{3}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\cdot (9-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{3}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\cdot (9-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358213 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc1fbb99-83e1-52ba-83f1-367de07351a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Improper Integral Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2cdade1-bc33-5b7c-b6b7-8a2436e23303" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3\\ln(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358215} {:answer/id #uuid "bcaa8e6f-2528-5646-b994-246d4c47151e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2cdade1-bc33-5b7c-b6b7-8a2436e23303" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3\\ln(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358215} :answer-field/id #uuid "c960562e-66f4-5a39-8f69-70987c9f9b6e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358214}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e50983a7-5268-5d47-b86f-cc6d21c90477" :question/math-academy-id "q-346618" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{3}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u={x}^{2}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{3}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{5}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{5}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{5}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[{5}^{u}]_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y={5}^{a},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{5}^{a}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067475/assets/c0c7699b036ebd8779cd6c3d3143cdcca6312834ee731e144e38488124164f43.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317072 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e84d5ab0-cec4-5988-9db3-8e296590d279" :knowledge-point/title "Improper Integral Involving Other Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0945e89d-40a1-5bbd-947b-87ccb466c405" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\ln(4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317074} {:answer/id #uuid "d06b5910-98c0-53be-a28f-dad13df40442" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334588}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0945e89d-40a1-5bbd-947b-87ccb466c405" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\ln(4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317074} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa485675-6e54-5354-a5be-95b88c6790a9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317073}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0aeedd54-c10a-53bd-b367-88f50ec08852" :question/math-academy-id "q-351320" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {4}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {4}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}3\\cdot {4}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{3\\cdot {4}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[3\\cdot {4}^{x}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(3-3\\cdot {4}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\cdot (3-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }3\\cdot {4}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={4}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{4}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{4}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067475/assets/fd4020291b348d130fc34969d72ff683aab5b6fb95ea5f7d32b90e5245d56d9c.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {4}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\cdot (3-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }3\\cdot {4}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\cdot (3-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334589 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc1fbb99-83e1-52ba-83f1-367de07351a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Improper Integral Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "427f5e75-f008-5509-bb6f-c1b5a13aa4f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\ln(6)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334591}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "427f5e75-f008-5509-bb6f-c1b5a13aa4f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\ln(6)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334591} :answer-field/id #uuid "454dc733-daaa-538f-850b-a61b7bf3568f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334590}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "13cadb7c-3bc6-5457-8b82-42f904d7adf6" :question/math-academy-id "q-351511" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{6}^{-2\\sqrt{x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=\\sqrt{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,⟹\\,2\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\sqrt{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{6}^{-2\\sqrt{x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{6}^{-2u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{6}^{-2\\sqrt{x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{6}^{-2u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{6}^{-2u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{6}^{-2u}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{6}^{-2u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{6}^{2a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{6}^{2x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{6}^{2x}}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{6}^{2x}}=0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067475/assets/8f732515dd376db40889706419781e02cbedfcd4da8513a09e4f2d77ff88ece2.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{6}^{-2\\sqrt{x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{6}^{2a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
